# FIELD OF DISCLOSURE

1.  The present disclosure generally relates to large language models
    (LLMs), and more particularly, to a method and system for symbolic
    reasoning with resonance in the LLMs, which further enables
    extensions toward Generative Intelligence applications.

# BACKGROUND

2.  Conventional transformer-based large language models (LLMs) such as
    GPT and Claude operate through dense attention and probabilistic
    token prediction. These architectures generate fluent responses but
    lack symbolic scaffolding and explicit Vritti (mental state)
    modeling, recursive ontology flows, or folded truth resurfacing.

3.  As a result, these systems exhibit persistent limitations: (i)
    hallucination, where outputs deviate from factual content; (ii)
    inefficiency from reliance on full-context attention; (iii)
    inability to adjust delivery tone or emotional resonance; and (iv)
    limited explainability, since confidence appears only as logit
    spread rather than structured internal variables such as aspects
    (symbolic dimensional ontology), Guna (energy modes), and Kosha
    (awareness layers) entropies.

4.  Existing approaches such as retrieval augmentation, prompt
    engineering, and parameter scaling improve surface performance but
    remain confined to probabilistic methods. These approaches do not
    introduce structured ontologies, layered feedback, or symbolic
    purification, and thus cannot trace causes and consequences across
    reasoning layers or align outputs with readiness. They further lack
    governance gates such as domain entropy gating, cross-domain entropy
    gating, and Kosha readiness gating that ensure stability and
    symbolic fidelity.

5.  Accordingly, it is desired to have a framework that enables more
    stable, explainable reasoning, reduces hallucination, improves
    efficiency, and provides auditable internal states. Such a framework
    should incorporate syllable--Vritti decomposition, ontology mapping,
    entropy feedback across multiple dimensions, and runtime mechanisms
    such as mirror logic, experiential anchors, and a Deferred Insight
    Engine to resurface unresolved truths only under readiness
    conditions.

6.  By combining symbolic decomposition with entropy-driven control, the
    invention advances beyond conventional LLMs toward systems that are
    both technically grounded and capable of adaptive delivery through
    dual reasoning flows that balance executional clarity with recursive
    depth. This invention therefore introduces a symbolic reasoning
    architecture that integrates structured ontologies, entropy
    feedback, and governance mechanisms to deliver stable, transparent,
    and context-aligned outputs beyond the limits of transformer models.

BRIEF SUMMARY OF INVENTION

7.  This brief overview is provided to introduce a selection of concepts
    in a simplified form that are further described below in the
    Detailed Description. This brief overview is not intended to
    identify key features or essential features of the claimed subject
    matter. Nor is this brief overview intended to be used to limit the
    claimed subject matter's scope.

8.  The disclosed system, Symbol-U, addresses the above prior art
    limitations by introducing a symbolic resonance framework. Words are
    decomposed into syllables, each mapped to Vritti (mental state)
    distributions. In one embodiment, the Vritti categories follow five
    classical states: valid cognition, conceptual construction,
    misperception, inertness, and memory. These states provide a
    symbolic space for entropy measurement and recursive processing.

9.  The disclosed system further employs: (i) a ten-layered aspects
    reasoning stack modeled as symbolic dimensions; (ii) entropy engines
    computing dimensional and Guna entropies to assess stability; (iii)
    a stitching encoder with redundancy and domain-jump penalties; (iv)
    runtime hotfix toggles with beam-expansion triggers and tonal
    harmonization via a Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA); (v) a
    Deferred Insight Engine to resurface content under readiness
    conditions; and (vi) experiential anchors with hysteresis for
    grounding. Unlike purely probabilistic models, Symbol-U integrates
    symbolic recursion, entropy feedback, and experiential anchoring.

10. This framework enables more stable, explainable reasoning, reduces
    hallucination, improves efficiency, and provides auditable internal
    states. By combining symbolic decomposition with entropy-driven
    control, the invention advances beyond conventional LLMs toward
    systems that are both technically grounded and capable of adaptive
    delivery. In certain embodiments, this framework further serves as a
    kernel for Generative Intelligence applications, where symbolic
    reasoning, entropy modulation, and deferred insight provide the
    foundations of general intelligence beyond probabilistic language
    modeling.

11. One embodiment of the present disclosure provides a system for
    symbolic reasoning in a language model including a processor of a
    symbolic reasoning (SR) node configured to host a candidate
    generation module and connected to at least one user entity node
    over a network and a memory on which are stored machine-readable
    instructions that when executed by the processor, cause the
    processor to: decompose an input text provided by the at least one
    user entity node into syllabic or phonetic units; associate each
    unit with one or more mental state distributions comprising Vrittis;
    map the units into an ontology of reasoning layers; compute entropy
    values over symbolic aspects and Guna states to assess stability;
    and generate one or more candidate responses using the candidate
    generation module.

12. Another embodiment of the present disclosure provides a method for
    symbolic reasoning in a language model including: decomposing an
    input text provided by the at least one user entity node into
    syllabic or phonetic units; associating each unit with one or more
    mental state distributions comprising Vrittis; mapping the units
    into an ontology of reasoning layers; computing entropy values over
    symbolic aspects and Guna states to assess stability; and generating
    one or more candidate responses using the candidate generation
    module.

13. Another embodiment of the present disclosure provides a
    computer-readable medium including instructions for: decomposing an
    input text provided by the at least one user entity node into
    syllabic or phonetic units; associating each unit with one or more
    mental state distributions comprising Vrittis; mapping the units
    into an ontology of reasoning layers; computing entropy values over
