29. The present disclosure includes many aspects and features. Moreover,
    while many aspects and features relate to, and are described in the
    context of symbolic reasoning with resonance in the LLMs and hybrid
    symbolic--probabilistic systems, embodiments of the present
    disclosure are not limited to use only in this context.

30. The present disclosure provides a system, method, and
    computer-readable medium for a symbolic language model (Symbol-U)
    that integrates syllabic decomposition, ontology mapping, and
    entropy feedback to improve stability, explainability, and grounding
    in large language models (LLMs). Input text is decomposed into
    syllables, each associated with Vritti (mental state) distributions
    such as valid cognition, imagination, misperception, unconscious
    state, and memory. Units are mapped into a ten-layer ontology, and
    entropy values are computed across dimensional, guna, and kosha
    layers to assess stability and symbolic alignment. A stitching
    encoder scores candidate responses with penalties, while runtime
    toggles regulate thresholds and tonal harmonization through a
    Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA). Recursive ontology flows
    provide self-correction, while experiential anchors and a deferred
    insight engine resurface unresolved content under readiness
    conditions.

31. A mirror-logic operation reflects or inverts aspect weights to
    expose hidden distortions and reinforce resonance alignment.
    Governance includes domain entropy gating, cross-domain entropy
    gating, and Kosha readiness gating to ensure symbolic fidelity. Dual
    reasoning flows balance executional clarity with recursive depth,
    and recursion states and entropy values are recorded in an
    append-only log with optional tamper-resistant chaining for
    transparency and compliance.

32. By leveraging these capabilities of the symbolic language model, the
    disclosed approach offers a significant improvement over existing
    solutions discussed above in the background section.

33. Glossary - The following definitions are used herein.

34. Vritti --- As used herein, "Vritti" refers to a symbolic cognitive
    or emotional state distribution within language reasoning. In one
    embodiment, Vritti categories are defined as valid cognition,
    misperception, imagination, unconscious state, and memory. For
    reference, these conceptually correspond to categories sometimes
    described in classical philosophy as Pramana, Viparyaya, Vikalpa,
    Nidra, and Smrti; however, for the purposes of the present
    disclosure, the English terms are controlling.

35. Guna --- As used herein, "guna" refers to a quality or energy mode.
    In the system, guna entropy represents distributions across three
    states: clarity and balance, activity and desire, and inertia or
    stillness. For reference, these conceptually correspond to
    categories sometimes described in classical philosophy as sattva,
    rajas, and tamas; however, for the purposes of the present
    disclosure, the English terms are controlling. These states provide
    polarity for resonance feedback and symbolic regulation.

36. Kosha --- As used herein, "Kosha" refers to an awareness layer or
    sheath of consciousness. The system defines five Koshas, structured
    as horizontal layers: Physical, Vital, Emotional, Intellectual, and
    Spiritual. These Koshas provide a framework for symbolic mapping and
    recursive processing.

37. Symbolic Dimensional Aspects --- There are 10 aspects, mapped to a
    vertical hierarchy of symbolic reasoning dimensions: Acting,
    Tagging, Forming, Thinking, Directing, Reasoning, Purposing,
    Meta-Observing, Unifying, and Absolving.

38. Deferred Insights Engine --- memory construct for deferred insights,
    resurfaced only when entropy and readiness conditions properly
    align.

39. Symbolic Resonance --- the alignment of syllable-level acoustic
    intent, Vritti states, and ontology mapping for coherent meaning.

40. Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA) --- a module that selects
    tonal delivery (Sweet Resonance, Inverse Jolt, or Symbolic Metaphor)
    based on readiness, resistance, and compliance gates.

41. Recursive Ontology Flows --- upward and downward symbolic traversals
    across the ten-layer reasoning stack, modulated by entropy feedback,
    folded truth resurfacing, and resonance alignment.

42. Experience Anchors --- experiential filters corresponding to human
    categories (Needs, Exchange, Belonging, Expression, Challenge,
    Relation, Change, Meaning, Role, Collective) that stabilize
    recursion and ground symbolic flows.

43. Hybrid Switching --- an adaptive mechanism that alternates between
    symbolic recursion and experiential anchor recursion when entropy
    thresholds indicate instability, resuming symbolic recursion upon
    stabilization.

44. **Pipeline**

45. In one embodiment, a request is processed through a pipeline
    including: (a) Syllable--Vritti processing to generate aspect and
    Vritti distributions; (b) Vritti categories defined as valid
    cognition, imagination, misperception, unconscious state, and
    memory (c) Ontology mapping into ten reasoning layers; (d) Entropy
    engines computing dimensional, Guna and Kosha entropies; (e)
    Stitching encoder and objective with redundancy and domain-jump
    penalties; (f) Runtime hotfix toggles and tonal harmonization to
    regulate stability and delivery; (g) Recursive ontology flows with
    entropy-based self-correction and convergence; (h) Experience
    anchors and hybrid switching that stabilize reasoning via
    hysteresis; (i) Governance gates for harm, time, resonance,
    provenance, and compliance; (j) Deferred Insight Engine to resurface
    unresolved content when readiness is satisfied; (k Delivery
    Harmonization Algorithm (DHA) selecting tone modes; (l)
    Explainability logging in an append-only log, with optional
    tamper-resistant extensions secured against retroactive alteration.

46. The key advantages of the disclosed system are: (a) Integration of
    symbolic and probabilistic reasoning for stability under
    uncertainty; (b) Explicit Vritti categories enabling structured
    cognitive state tracking; (c) Governance gates and tamper-resistant
    logs for compliance and auditability; (d) Delivery harmonization
    aligning tone with user readiness; (e) Mirror logic on dimensional
    ontology to expose distortion and reinforce resonance alignment; (f)
    Balancing of dual reasoning flows, internal and external, to deliver
    executional clarity while maintaining recursive depth.

47. In one embodiment, a recursive symbolic reasoning system employs
    syllabic decomposition, aspect mapping, entropy modulation, Deferred
    Insights Engine, and a stitching encoder with penalties and toggles.
    A Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA) modulates tone: Sweet
    Resonance, Inverse Jolt, or Symbolic Metaphor, selected adaptively
    based on entropy thresholds and readiness gates.

48. **Syllable--Vritti processing to generate aspect and Vritti
    distributions**

49. In one embodiment, input text is decomposed into pronounceable
    syllabic or phonetic units. Each unit is associated with one or more
    Vritti distributions, representing positive and negative mental
    state pathways. Consonants are modeled as carriers of distortion
    (negative Vritti) and purification pathways (positive Vritti).
    Vowels serve as bridges between adjacent reasoning aspects, defining
    directional movement and transitions in consciousness. In some
    embodiments vowels act as transition gates between ontology layers.

50. Let the input string $W$ be segmented into a sequence of syllables:

$$W \rightarrow (s_{1},s_{2},\ldots,s_{n}).$$

51. For each syllable $s_{i}$, a distribution over Vritti states may be
    computed, for example as:

$$p_{v}\lbrack v \mid s_{i}\rbrack,\quad v \in V_{\text{Vritti }}.$$

52. For clarity, let $V_{\text{Vritti }}$ denote the set of Vritti
    states and let $G$ denote the set of Guna states. The system may
    write $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ for Vritti probabilities with
    $v \in V_{\text{Vritti }}$, and $p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack$ for Guna
    probabilities with $g \in G$. In some embodiments, an additional
    $p_{k}\lbrack k\rbrack$ distribution is defined over Kosha states
    $k \in K$ for readiness assessment.

53. In certain embodiments, entropy measures as defined in the claims
    may be applied at this level, such as:

$$H_{D} = - \sum_{a \in A}^{}p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack \cdot logp_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack,\quad H_{G} = - \sum_{g \in G}^{}p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack \cdot logp_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack,\quad H_{K} = - \sum_{k \in K}^{}p_{k}\lbrack k\rbrack \cdot logp_{k}\lbrack k\rbrack.$$

54. where: -- $p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack$ denotes a probability distribution
    over symbolic aspects $a \in A$; -- $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ denotes
    a probability distribution over Vritti states
    $v \in V_{\text{Vritti }}$; -- $p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack$ denotes a
    probability distribution over Guna states $g \in G$; --
    $p_{k}\lbrack k\rbrack$ denotes a probability distribution over
    Kosha states $k \in K$. Note that mathematically equivalent forms of
    $H_{D}$, $H_{G}$, and $H_{K}$ may also be used without limitation.

55. The following word-level example illustrates the above processing.
    Consider the input word *"sad"*, decomposed as
    $\lbrack sa,da\rbrack$. The syllable "sa" expresses escapism/static
    detachment (negative Vritti), while "da" expresses
    forgiveness/patience (positive Vritti ). Aspect weights and Vritti
    coupling may yield dimensional entropy $H_{D} \approx 0.95$ and Guna
    entropy $H_{G} \approx 0.72$, with Kosha entropy $H_{K}$ further
    reflecting readiness alignment. Based on these values, the Delivery
    Harmonization Algorithm (DHA) selects a *Symbolic Metaphor* output
    (e.g., "a heavy sky before it clears") appropriate to user
    readiness. Based on these values, the Delivery Harmonization
    Algorithm (DHA) selects a Symbolic Metaphor output (e.g., "a heavy
    sky before it clears") appropriate to user readiness.

56. Vritti categories

57. Enumerated Vritti categories may be implemented as follows. In one
    embodiment, the Vritti distributions explicitly include symbolic
    mental state categories defined as: (a) valid cognition or direct
    knowledge, (b) conceptual construction or imagination, (c)
    distortion or misperception, (d) inertness or sleep-like
    non-awareness, and (e) memory or recall.

58. These categories serve as formal states within $\mathcal{V}$, each
    represented as a weighted probability contributing to entropy
    calculations and symbolic recursion flows.

59. **Ontology mapping into ten reasoning layers**

60. Aspect Mapping may be implemented as follows. In one embodiment
    syllabic features and Vritti distributions are mapped into a
    ten-layer symbolic ontology of reasoning aspects. Each aspect
    represents a distinct dimension of symbolic reasoning, aligned to
    traditional ontological categories.

61. The ontology layers may, in certain embodiments, be defined as:

$$\begin{matrix}
\mathcal{O = \{}\text{Acting},\ \text{Tagging},\ \text{Forming},\ \text{Thinking},\ \text{Directing}, \\
\text{Reasoning},\ \text{Purposing},\ \text{Observing},\ \text{Unifying},\ \text{Absolute}\}.
\end{matrix}$$

62. Each syllable $s_{j}$ may contribute to aspect weights, for example
    as:

$$p_{w}^{a}\lbrack s_{j}\rbrack,\quad a\mathcal{\in L}$$

63. where $p_{w}^{a}\lbrack s_{j}\rbrack$ denotes the probability of
    aspect $a$ being activated by syllable $s_{j}$. Other weighting
    functions or equivalent probability measures may also be used.

In some embodiments, a context--Vritti coupling coefficient is computed
to measure alignment between Vritti states and aspect distributions,
such as:

$$s_{C} = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}_{vritti}}^{}P_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack \cdot \kappa(v,C)$$

64. where: (a) $P_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ is the probability of Vritti
    state $v$; (b) $C$ represents the symbolic or semantic context of
    the input; (c) $\kappa(v,C)$ is a similar function between Vritti
    state $v$ and context $C$. Equivalent similarity or alignment
    functions may be substituted without limitation.

65. Objective Mapping may be implemented as follows. Aspect weights may
    be further used in the penalized objective defined in Claim 3, where
    the subset $S^{*}$ of candidate aspects or responses is selected,
    for example as:

$$S^{*} = arg\max_{S,\mspace{6mu}\text{order}}\sum_{i \in S}^{}\text{rel}_{i} - \lambda_{1}\,\text{red}(S) - \lambda_{2}\, dj(S)$$

subject to constraints
$|S| \leq K,\mspace{6mu} len \leq L,\mspace{6mu}\text{rel}_{i} \geq \tau_{rel}$.
Other mathematically equivalent optimization objectives may also be
employed.

66. Example of Aspect Alignment is provided. For the sentence "*I need
    comfort, safety, or reassurance*", fragments map to the aspects
    Purposing, Thinking, and Observing. Aspect weights combine with
    Vritti distributions to yield scores that guide recursive ontology
    traversal and candidate scoring.

67. **Entropy engines: Dimensional, Guna, and Kosha entropies**

68. Entropy Engines may be implemented as follows. In one embodiment,
    dimensional entropy, Guna entropy, and resonance modulation
    coefficients are computed to assess system stability and modulate
    recursion confidence.

69. Dimensional Entropy is implemented as follows. In certain
    embodiments, dimensional entropy may be computed over aspect
    distributions as:

$$H_{D} = - \sum_{a \in A}^{}P_{w}^{a}\lbrack s\rbrack \cdot logP_{w}^{a}\lbrack s\rbrack$$

70. where: a) $A$ is the set of symbolic aspects, (b)
    $P_{w}^{a}\lbrack s\rbrack$ is the probability of aspect $a$ derived
    from syllable mappings. Note that other mathematically equivalent
    entropy measures may also be employed.

71. Guna Entropy may be implemented as follows. In certain embodiments,
    Guna entropy may be computed over Guna distributions as:

$$H_{G} = - \sum_{g \in G}^{}P_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack \cdot logP_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack$$

72. where: a) $G$ is the set of Guna states; and (b)
    $P_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack$ is the probability of Guna state $g$.\
    Alternative formulations of entropy across Guna states may be
    substituted without limitation.

73. Kosha Entropy may be implemented as follows. A probability
    distribution is defined over Koshas $P_{K}\lbrack k\rbrack$ and
    Kosha entropy is calculated:

$$H_{K} = - \sum_{k \in K}^{}P_{K}\lbrack k\rbrack \cdot logP_{K}\lbrack k\rbrack\quad\quad\text{with}\quad\quad{\widetilde{H}}_{K} = \frac{H_{K}}{log|K|} \in \lbrack 0,1\rbrack.$$

74. where: a) $K$ is the set of Kosha states; and (b)
    $P_{K}\lbrack k\rbrack$ is the probability distribution over Kosha
    states.

75. Entropy Modulation is implemented as follows. Recursion confidence
    may be adjusted dynamically using entropy-driven modulation
    functions, for example:

$$\begin{aligned}
\alpha^{'} & = \alpha \cdot \frac{1}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{D} - \tau_{D})}} \\
\beta^{'} & = \beta \cdot \frac{1}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{G} - \tau_{G})}} \\
\gamma^{'} & = \gamma \cdot \frac{1}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{K} - \tau_{K})}}
\end{aligned}$$

76. where: (a) $\alpha^{'},\beta^{'},\gamma^{'}$ are adjusted recursion
    weights, (b) $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ are base scaling parameters, (c)
    $\tau_{D},\tau_{G},\tau_{K}$ are entropy thresholds, (d) $\kappa$ is
    a sharpness parameter controlling sigmoid steepness. Equivalent
    monotonic or saturating functions may also be used to achieve
    similar modulation effects.

77. Resonance Modulation Coefficient may be implemented as follows. In
    one embodiment, entropy values including dimensional $H_{D}$, Guna
    $H_{G}$, and kosha $H_{K}$ are further scaled by a resonance
    modulation coefficient to regulate recursion confidence and symbolic
    stability.

78. The final expression gate may then be written as:

$$\text{Expression}(FT) = f(H_{D}^{P},H_{G}^{G},H_{K}^{R}(k))$$

79. Application may be implemented as follows. The values
    $H_{D}^{P},H_{G}^{G},H_{K}^{R},\alpha^{'},\beta^{'},\gamma^{'}$ and
    $\lambda_{res}$ are combined to decide whether to continue symbolic
    recursion, trigger anchor switching, or surface deferred insights.
    Unless stated otherwise, $\log$ denotes the natural logarithm; any
    positive base yields a constant-factor scaling immaterially to the
    claimed methods.

80. **Stitching encoder and objective with redundancy and domain-jump
    penalties**.

81. In one embodiment, candidate responses are combined using a
    stitching encoder that selects and orders a subset $S^{*}$ of
    candidate entries to maximize symbolic relevance while minimizing
    distortion. The encoder balances three objectives:

82. **Relevance:** Candidates are scored by a multi-factor relevance
    score:

    $$\text{rel}_{i} = p_{w}\lbrack a_{i}\rbrack^{\theta_{1}} \cdot (\sum_{v \in V}^{}p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack \cdot R\lbrack v,a_{i}\rbrack)^{\theta_{2}} \cdot \phi_{d}(d_{i}|a_{i})^{\theta_{3}} \cdot \phi_{t}(t_{i}|p_{v})^{\theta_{4}} \cdot c^{\theta_{5}}$$

83. where: $p_{w}\lbrack a_{i}\rbrack$ = normalized aspect weight for
    aspect $a_{i} \in \{\text{Action} \rightarrow \text{Absolute}\}$;
    $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ = Vritti probabilities;
    $R\lbrack v,a_{i}\rbrack$ = Vritti--aspect coupling matrix;
    $\phi_{d}(d_{i}|a_{i})$ = domain fit score\
    $\phi_{t}(t_{i}|p_{v})$ = template fit softmax;
    $c = f(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$ = confidence derived from dimensional,
    Guna, and Kosha entropies; $\theta$ = weight vector modulated by
    complexity metric $Q^{'}$

84. **Redundancy Penalty:** To discourage repetition, a redundancy score
    is applied:

    $$\text{red}(S) = \sum_{i < j \in S}^{}\left( \alpha_{\text{sem}} \cdot cos(e_{i},e_{j}) + \alpha_{\text{asp}} \cdot \mathbf{1}(a_{i} = a_{j}) + \alpha_{\text{tmp}} \cdot \mathbf{1}(t_{i} = t_{j}) \right)$$

85. where cosine similarity and indicator functions measure semantic,
    aspect, and template overlap.

86. **Domain-Jump Penalty:** Coherence is maintained by penalizing
    abrupt transitions:

    $$dj(S) = \sum_{i \rightarrow j \in \text{order}(S)}^{}D(d_{i},d_{j}),$$

87. where $D(d_{i},d_{j})$ measures cross-domain distance dynamically
    adjusted by resonance and Vritti balance.

88. The stitching objective is then:

    $$S^{*} = arg\max_{S,order}\left\lbrack \sum_{i \in S}^{}\text{rel}_{i}\mspace{6mu} - \mspace{6mu}\lambda_{1} \cdot \text{red}(S)\mspace{6mu} - \mspace{6mu}\lambda_{2} \cdot dj(S) \right\rbrack,$$

89. subject to length and confidence constraints. Penalty weights
    $\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}$ are bound to prevent verbosity and may
    adapt based on resonance or contextual entropy.

90. This encoder ensures that final responses are *coherent, concise,
    ontologically aligned*, and free of excessive redundancy or
    incoherent domain shifts. In practice, redundancy penalties preserve
    diversity of reasoning, while domain-jump penalties ensure that
    symbolic recursion flows smoothly across ontology layers.

91. **Runtime Hotfix Toggles**

92. In some embodiments, the system adaptively adjusts entropy
    thresholds and quality metrics to improve efficiency and stability
    under varying entropy conditions. The adjusted quality score $Q^{'}$
    is computed as:

$$Q^{'} = Q_{\text{base}} + bump,$$

where the bump term is defined as:

$$\tau_{\text{hedge}} = clip\left( \alpha \cdot H_{D} + \beta \cdot H_{G} + \gamma \cdot H_{K} + \zeta \cdot H_{R},\tau_{\min},\tau_{\max} \right)$$

93. where $Q_{\text{base}}$ is a baseline quality measure, $bump$ is an
    adjustment term, $H_{D}$ is a dimensional entropy value, $H_{G}$ is
    a Guna entropy value, $H_{K}$ is a Kosha entropy value and $H_{R}$
    is a resonance entropy and $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$, $\zeta$,
    $\tau_{\min}$, and $\tau_{\max}$ are tunable parameters.

94. Expanded candidate generation is triggered when entropy values
    exceed thresholds, such that

$$Trigger = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }H_{D} > \tau_{D}\mspace{6mu}\text{or}\mspace{6mu} H_{G} > \tau_{G}\mspace{6mu}\text{or}\mspace{6mu} H_{K} > \tau_{K}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

95. where $H_{D}$, $H_{G}$, and $H_{K}$ are dimensional, Guna, and Kosha
    entropy values, and $\tau_{D}$, $\tau_{G}$, and $\tau_{K}$ are their
    respective thresholds.

96. By capping or relaxing thresholds based on context, the toggles
    prevent unnecessary recursion in stable states, while enabling
    additional candidate exploration during unstable states. This
    dynamic adjustment balances computational efficiency with symbolic
    robustness.

97. Equivalent monotonic or saturating functions may be substituted for
    the bump term without limitation, provided they preserve the
    stabilizing effect. Toggle parameters may also be configured
    per-domain or globally, depending on deployment constraints.

98. **Implementation with Hotfix Toggles**

99. In some embodiments, the stitching encoder operates under adaptive
    conditions regulated by runtime hotfix toggles. The toggles modify
    candidate selection parameters, ensuring both computational
    efficiency and symbolic robustness:

100. Beam Candidate Cap: When entropy exceeds thresholds, beam search is
     invoked but capped at a maximum number of candidates ($\leq 20$),
     preventing exponential candidate growth.

101. Hedging Threshold: If entropy confidence $f(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$
     falls below $\tau_{hedge}$, the stitching encoder injects cautious
     templates or hedge terms into candidates, ensuring stability of
     delivery.

102. Slope Controls: The Imaginative floor slope (0.6--0.8 range) is
     adjusted to prevent premature pruning of imaginative candidates
     during exploration, while nuanced $\beta$-scaling modulates
     template diversity versus certainty.

103. Deterministic $Q^{'}$ Mode: In deterministic toggle mode,
     entropy-weighted coefficients ($\theta_{1},\theta_{2},\theta_{3}$)
     are replaced with fixed values, enabling low-latency candidate
     stitching for enterprise deployments.

104. Resonance-Conditioned Linking: If resonance score $\lambda_{res}$
     is high, the stitching encoder enforces a forced link template,
     ensuring continuity across domain transitions rather than pruning
     them as domain jumps.\
     Together, these toggle-driven controls prevent hallucination,
     reduce redundant branching, and adaptively rebalance the stitching
     process under variable entropy conditions

105. **Deferred Insight Engine**

106. In one embodiment, unresolved symbolic distortions are deferred
     into a memory structure and resurfaced when readiness conditions
     are satisfied. Deferred insights may be reframed through
     experiential anchors prior to reintegration with each resurfacing
     recorded for audit.

107. Deferral Symbolic content that cannot be immediately resolved may
     be stored in a deferred insight store. Each item is tracked with
     lifecycle flags, for example:

$$\{ is\_ active,\mspace{6mu} is\_ ready\_ to\_ surface,\mspace{6mu} surfaced\_ count,\mspace{6mu} last\_ surface\_ time\}$$

108. Readiness Scoring is implemented as follows. An item may be
     eligible for resurfacing when its readiness score exceeds a
     threshold, such as:

$$R_{item} = \gamma H_{D}^{D} + \delta H_{G}^{G} + \eta H_{K}^{K} + \nu t\quad > \quad\tau_{R}$$

109. where: (a) $R_{item}$ is the readiness score; (b)
     $H_{D}^{D},H_{G}^{G}$ are dimensional and Guna entropy; $H_{K}^{K}$
     is Kosha entropy (if defined); (c) $t$ is elapsed time since
     deferral; (d) $\gamma,\delta,\eta,\nu$ are weighting
     parameters; (e) $\tau_{R}$ is a readiness threshold.

110. Anchor-Based Reframing is implemented as follows. When resurfaced,
     deferred items may be reframed by anchor conditioning, for example:

$$re{l^{'}}_{j} = rel_{j}^{i} \cdot \phi_{anchor}(a,c_{j}) \cdot \phi_{mirror}(m,c_{j})$$

111. where: (a) $re{l^{'}}_{j}$ is the adjusted relevance score; (b)
     $rel_{j}^{i}$ is the baseline relevance score of candidate
     $c_{j}$; (c) $a$ is the selected experiential anchor; (d)
     $\phi_{anchor}(a,c_{j})$ is an anchor influence function that
     modifies candidate $c_{j}$ relevance according to anchor $a$;
     and (e) $\phi_{mirror}(m,c_{j})$ reflects/inverts aspect weights
     when mirror-logic is active $(m = 1)$ to expose distortions.\
     Alternative scaling or conditioning functions may be used without
     limitation, provided $\phi$ are bounded and monotone in their
     respective confidence arguments.

112. Lifecycle is implemented as follows. Deferred insights cycle
     between active storage and resurfacing until fully resolved or
     dismissed by governance gates; terminal states include {resolved,
     expired}.

113. In some embodiments, the deferred insight engine further comprises
     a triadic balancing framework that regulates resurfacing of
     deferred truths. A unifying force applies progressive pressure to
     eventually surface deferred insights, while an observing restraint
     defers or withholds resurfacing until readiness thresholds are
     satisfied. In addition, mirror logic operates as a reflective
     buffer, providing partial, symbolic, or preview-style delivery of
     insights prior to full release. This triadic mechanism ensures that
     deferred truths are neither prematurely suppressed nor
     destructively surfaced but instead reintroduced in a balanced
     manner through reflective previews, symbolic metaphors, or gradual
     escalation in alignment with readiness scores and governance gates.
     In some embodiments, the triadic balancing framework further adapts
     to non-stationary objectives, wherein readiness scores and mirror
     logic outputs are dynamically recomputed as user intent, context
     entropy, or harm thresholds evolve over time, thereby allowing
     deferred truths to realign with shifting objectives prior to full
     resurfacing.

114. In some embodiments, resurfacing may be governed by the following
     algorithm: (a) Compute readiness score 𝑅 = 𝛼$H_{D}$ + 𝛽$H_{G}$ +
     𝛾$H_{K}$ + 𝛿𝑇. (b) If R \< $\tau_{\text{ready}}$→ Observing force
     defers resurfacing. (c) If 𝑅 ≥ $\tau_{\text{ready}}$but projected
     harm \> $\tau_{\text{harm}}$ → Mirror logic generates preview
     output. (d) If 𝑅 ≥ $\tau_{\text{ready}}$and harm ≤
     $\tau_{\text{harm}}$ → Unifying force triggers full resurfacing.

115. Where: $H_{D}$ denotes dimensional entropy, capturing uncertainty
     across symbolic dimensions. $H_{G}$ denotes Guna entropy, measuring
     stability across three modes of nature: clarity and balance,
     activity and desire, and inertia or obscurity. $H_{K}$ denotes
     kosha entropy, reflecting balance or distortion across experiential
     layers. 𝑇 denotes temporal factor, capturing readiness as a
     function of time evolution. 𝛼, 𝛽, 𝛾, 𝛿 denote weighting
     coefficients applied to respective entropies.
     $\tau_{\text{ready}}$denotes readiness threshold for safe
     resurfacing. $\tau_{\text{harm}}$denotes projected harm threshold
     for protective gating.

116. **Recursive Ontology Flows with entropy-based self-correction and
     convergence**

117. Recursive Ontology Flows and Hybrid Switching are implemented as
     follows. In one embodiment, recursion is applied across ontology
     layers and sub-layers, producing dual reasoning flows and adapting
     to entropy feedback until convergence or termination criteria are
     satisfied, with mirror-logic optionally engaged when distortion is
     detected.

118. The system may employ Dual Reasoning Flows. In some embodiments,
     two flows are maintained: (a) An outer execution-aligned flow
     producing candidate responses aligned to immediate task demands,
     and (b) An inner recursive flow performing symbolic refinement
     across ontology layers; a blending parameter
     $\alpha \in \lbrack 0,1\rbrack$ may combine the flows.

119. Sub-Layer Recursion is implemented as follows. Within each ontology
     layer $L$, recursion may be extended into sub-layers, for example:

$$x^{(k + 1)} = f\left( x_{L_{sub}}^{(k)},H_{D},H_{G},H_{K} \right)$$

120. where $x^{(k)}$ is the recursion state at iteration $k$, $L_{sub}$
     is a nested sub-layer, and $H_{D},H_{G},H_{K}$ are entropies as
     defined in Claim 1 and related embodiments. Equivalent recursion
     update functions may also be applied.

121. Self-Correcting Loop may be implemented as follows. Recursion may
     be modulated by entropy feedback to reduce deviation, such as:

$$x^{(k + 1)} = f\left( x^{(k)} - \eta\nabla E_{x}^{(k)} \right)$$

122. where the entropy error may be expressed as:

$$E_{x}^{(k)} = \alpha H_{D} + \beta H_{G} + \gamma H_{K} + \tau_{D}/H_{D} + \tau_{G}/H_{G} + \tau_{K}/H_{K}$$

123. with $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ as weighting coefficients and $\eta$ as
     a correction rate. Other mathematically equivalent correction rules
     may be used without limitation.

124. Convergence Criteria is implemented as follows. Recursive loops may
     terminate when either convergence or iteration limits are reached,
     for example:

$$\Delta(x^{(k)}) = \parallel x^{(k + 1)} - x^{(k)} \parallel \leq \epsilon,\quad\text{or}\quad k \geq K_{\max}$$

125. where $\epsilon$ is a convergence tolerance and $K_{\max}$ is the
     maximum number of iterations. In one embodiment, cross-domain
     generalization may be achieved by sharing symbolic parameters
     across domains, optionally with domain-specific adapters and
     smoothing priors.

126. Hybrid Switching is implemented as follows. When entropy thresholds
     are exceeded, recursion may be temporarily suspended and
     experiential anchors engaged. Symbolic recursion once entropy
     stabilizes; hysteresis prevents rapid oscillation.

127. **Experience Anchors and Activation Thresholds**

128. In one embodiment, experiential anchors may provide stabilizing
     reference points when symbolic recursion becomes unstable. Anchors
     correspond to categories of human experience and may be activated
     according to entropy thresholds with hysteresis control.

129. Anchor Library is implemented as follows. In certain embodiments, a
     library $\mathcal{A}$ of experiential categories is maintained, for
     example:

$$\mathcal{A =}\left\{ \begin{aligned}
 & \text{Needs}, \\
 & \text{Exchange}, \\
 & \text{Belonging}, \\
 & \text{Expression}, \\
 & \text{Challenge}, \\
 & \text{Relation}, \\
 & \text{Change}, \\
 & \text{Meaning}, \\
 & \text{Role}, \\
 & \text{Collective}
\end{aligned} \right\}$$

130. Anchor selection may be performed by:

$$anchor = arg\max_{a\mathcal{\in A}}\psi(a,H_{D},H_{G},H_{K},C)$$

131. where:(a) $anchor$ is the selected experiential anchor,\
     (b) $\psi(a,H_{D},H_{G},H_{K},C)$ is a scoring function based on
     entropies $H_{D},H_{G},H_{K}$ and symbolic context $C$. Other
     scoring functions or equivalent selection mechanisms may also be
     used including multi-armed bandit selection with entropy-aware
     priors.

132. Activation Thresholds with Hysteresis is implemented.

133. Anchors may be activated or deactivated with hysteresis to prevent
     oscillation, e.g.

$$Activate(a) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }H_{D} > \tau_{D}^{\text{high}}\ \text{or}\ H_{G} > \tau_{G}^{\text{high}}\ \text{or}\ H_{K} > \tau_{K}^{\text{high}}, \\
0, & \text{if }H_{D} < \tau_{D}^{\text{low}}\ \text{and}\ H_{G} < \tau_{G}^{\text{low}}\ \text{and}\ H_{K} < \tau_{K}^{\text{low}}, \\
\text{previous state}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

134. where
     $\tau_{D}^{\text{high}},\tau_{G}^{\text{high}},\tau_{K}^{\text{high}}$
     are upper thresholds, and
     $\tau_{D}^{\text{low}},\tau_{G}^{\text{low}},\tau_{K}^{\text{low}}$
     are lower thresholds.

135. When active, the anchor modifies candidate scoring and deferred
     resurfacing, ensuring alignment with experiential categories and
     improving recursion stability, after which symbolic recursion
     resumes.

136. **Governance Gates and Compliance Logging.**

137. In one embodiment, candidate responses are subjected to governance
     gates prior to output. Gate outcomes are recorded in an
     append--only log to ensure explainability and regulatory
     compliance, with optional tamper--resistant extensions for audit
     integrity.

138. Ontological Gates may be implemented. In some embodiments, three
     primary gate checks are applied:

$$G_{\text{harm}}(c) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
0, & \text{if }risk(c) > \tau_{\text{harm}}, \\
1, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ \quad\quad G_{\text{time}}(c) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
0, & \text{if }t_{\text{age}}(c) > \tau_{\text{time}}, \\
1, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

$$G_{\text{res}}(c) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }\lambda_{\text{res}}(c) \geq \tau_{\text{res}}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ \quad\quad G_{\text{dom}}(a) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }H_{D}(a) \leq \tau_{\text{dom}}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

$$G_{\text{cross}}(a,b) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }H_{\chi}(a,b) \leq \tau_{\text{cross}}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ \quad\quad G_{\text{kosha}}(k) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }R^{K}(k) \geq \tau_{\text{kosha}}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

139. Here $risk(c)$ is a harm-risk score, $t_{\text{age}}(c)$ is elapsed
     time, $\lambda_{\text{res}}(c)$ is a resonance score, $H_{D}(a)$ is
     the entropy within domain $a$, $H_{\chi}(a,b)$ is the cross-entropy
     between domains $a$ and $b$, and $R^{K}(k)$ is the readiness score
     of Kosha $k$. Thresholds
     $\tau_{\text{harm}},\tau_{\text{time}},\tau_{\text{res}},\tau_{\text{dom}},\tau_{\text{cross}},\tau_{\text{kosha}}$
     are respective parameters. Other mathematically equivalent gating
     functions may also be applied.

140. Extended Governance may be implemented as follows. Governance may
     be extended with additional checks, for example:

$$G_{\text{prov}}(c) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }source(c) \in S_{\text{approved}}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

$$G_{\text{comp}}(c) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }policy(c)\text{ satisfies compliance constraints}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

141. Here $source(c)$ is a provenance identifier, $S_{\text{approved}}$
     is a set of approved sources, and $policy(c)$ denotes rules or
     metadata associated with candidate $c$.

142. In some embodiments, a domain entropy gate ensures stability within
     a single symbolic domain by suppressing reasoning flows if the
     entropy of that domain exceeds a threshold. A cross-domain entropy
     gate regulates transitions between domains, preventing uncontrolled
     propagation of high-entropy signals across unrelated symbolic
     contexts. A Kosha readiness gate evaluates the readiness of a Kosha
     layer before allowing a candidate response, thereby ensuring that
     reasoning steps are not advanced until the underlying Kosha state
     has reached a minimum readiness threshold. Together, these gates
     complement harm, time, and resonance gates to provide
     multi-dimensional control over symbolic reasoning.

143. Explainability Logging is implemented as follows. At each recursion
     step $t$, internal state variables may be recorded for transparency
     and auditability. The log may include
     $H_{K},H_{D},H_{G},\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\alpha^{'},\beta^{'},\gamma^{'},Q,\tau_{\text{hedge}}$,
     and an anomaly score $a(t)$, for example defined as:

$$a(t) = \left| x(t) - \bar{x}(\theta) \right|_{2}$$

144. where $x(t)$ is the current state vector and $\bar{x}(\theta)$ is
     an exponential moving average of past states. Provenance tuples
     $\{ model\_ ver,r_{set},data\_ stamp\}$ may also be appended along
     with gate outcomes and anchor selections.

145. **Delivery Harmonization** **Algorithm**

146. In one embodiment, a Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA)
     modulates the tone of final outputs based on entropy levels,
     readiness scores, and governance outcomes so that delivery matches
     detected user state while satisfying active gates.

147. Tone selection is implemented as follows. In certain embodiments,
     three tonal modes are supported: (a) Sweet Resonance, providing a
     balanced and harmonious delivery; (b) Inverse Jolt, providing a
     disruptive or grounding delivery; and (c) Symbolic Metaphor,
     providing an active or reframing delivery. Other tone categories or
     delivery modes may also be employed.

148. Mode Decision Function is implemented as follows. The tonal mode
     may be selected according to, for example:

$$Mode = arg\max_{m \in \{ SR,IJ,SM\}}\mspace{6mu}\mspace{6mu}\phi\left( m,\mspace{6mu} H_{D},\mspace{6mu} H_{G},\mspace{6mu} H_{K},\mspace{6mu} R_{item},\mspace{6mu} Gates(c),\mspace{6mu} anchor,\mspace{6mu} m_{logic} \right)$$

149. where: $H_{D}^{p},H_{G}^{p}$ are dimensional and Guna entropy
     values, $H_{K}$ is Kosha entropy (if defined). $R_{\text{item}}$ is
     a readiness score from deferred insight resurfacing.
     $G_{\text{ates}}(c)$ are governance outcomes. $\Phi( \cdot )$ is a
     mode-scoring function evaluating suitability of mode $m$; $anchor$
     is the selected experiential anchor and
     $m_{\text{logic}} \in \{ 0,1\}$ indicates whether mirror-logic is
     active.

150. Equivalent decision functions may also be used without limitation
     including SoftMax or threshold voting across modes.

151. Runtime Toggles may be executed as follows: Hotfix toggles may
     influence tonal modulation, for example:

$$\tau_{\text{hedge}} = \text{clip}\left( \alpha H_{D} + \beta H_{G} + \gamma H_{K},\,\tau_{\min}^{\tau},\,\tau_{\max}^{\tau} \right)$$

152. which adjusts hedging thresholds for delivery confidence, where
     $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\zeta$ are scaling parameters and
     $\tau_{\min}^{\tau},\tau_{\max}^{\tau}$ bound the range.
     $\gamma,\zeta$ weight Guna and Kosha contributions. Other hedging
     functions or adaptive scaling methods may also be applied. The DHA
     harmonizes symbolic recursion outputs with user readiness, anchor
     conditioning, and compliance constraints before final delivery,
     ensuring both interpretability and safety; optional beam-expansion
     triggers may be engaged when confidence falls below
     $\tau_{\text{hedge}}$.

153. **Explainability and Logging**

154. In one embodiment, all internal reasoning variables and governance
     outcomes may be recorded in an append-only log for purposes of
     transparency, auditability, and compliance.

155. Logged Variables are used as follows. The log may record recursion
     states, entropy values, anchor activations, and gate results, for
     example:

$$Log = \{\,\left( t,\mspace{6mu} x^{(k)},\mspace{6mu} H_{D},\mspace{6mu} H_{G},\mspace{6mu} H_{K},\mspace{6mu}\lambda_{res},\mspace{6mu} anchor,\mspace{6mu} Gates(c),\mspace{6mu} Mode,\mspace{6mu} m_{logic} \right)\,\}_{t}^{T}$$

156. where: (a) $t$ is a time index, (b) $x^{(k)}$ is the recursion
     state at iteration $k$, (c) $H_{D},H_{G}$ are dimensional and Guna
     entropy values; $H_{K}$ is Kosha entropy (if defined), (d)
     $\lambda_{res}$ is the resonance modulation coefficient, (e)
     $anchor$ is the selected experiential anchor, (f) $Gates(c)$
     denotes governance gate outcomes for candidate $c$, (g) $Mode$ is
     the selected tonal mode and $m_{logic}$ indicates mirror-logic
     activation. Other equivalent data structures or formats may also be
     used.

157. In terms of compliance, the log may serve two functions:\
     (a) *Internal explainability* --- enabling developers and auditors
     to trace reasoning flows, entropy dynamics, and anchor
     interventions. (b) *External compliance* --- ensuring outputs can
     be verified against regulatory requirements, provenance rules, and
     ethical standards, with optional external anchoring of log digests
     to a trusted timestamping service.

158. Alternative compliance tracking methods or security mechanisms may
     also be applied.

159. Tamper-Resistant Logging is implemented as follows. In some
     embodiments, the explainability log is further secured against
     tampering using cryptographic chaining, digital signatures, or
     equivalent integrity mechanisms, thereby ensuring immutability and
     preventing retroactive alteration of recorded variables, a
     Merkle-style hash over log blocks may be employed.

160. Optionally, a permissioned blockchain or distributed ledger can
     anchor log digests to provide an external timestamp and audit trail
     without exposing raw data.

161. As discussed above, the disclosed system may incorporate a
     blockchain or comparable technology to ensure the transparency and
     immutability of transactions, providing a secure and trustworthy
     platform. By embedding these advanced technologies, the disclosed
     automated system, advantageously, offers a sophisticated and secure
     solution while maintaining optionality for non-blockchain
     deployments.

162. **Multimodal** **Inputs**

163. In one embodiment, the input tokens may further comprise features
     beyond text, including acoustic signals, visual features, symbolic
     tags, or semantic embeddings. Each modality is processed by a
     modality-specific encoder $\phi_{m}$ to generate an embedding
     vector $x_{m}$, which may be normalized as:

$${\widetilde{x}}_{m} = \frac{x_{m}}{\parallel x_{m} \parallel}$$

164. A reliability score $q_{m}$ is computed per modality (e.g.,
     signal-to-noise ratio for audio, detection confidence for image, or
     perplexity for text). Normalized fusion weights are then computed
     using a softmax function:

$$\alpha_{m} = \frac{exp(\kappa q_{m})}{\sum_{m^{'}}^{}\exp(\kappa q_{m^{'}})}$$

165. The fused multimodal state is given by:

$$h = \sum_{m}^{}\alpha_{m}\,{\widetilde{x}}_{m}$$

166. The fused state vector is then projected into:

$$p_{v} = \text{softmax}(W_{v}h + b_{v}),\quad\quad p_{w} = \text{softmax}(W_{a}h + b_{a})$$

167. Here, $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ defines the Vritti probability
     distribution over classical mental states cognition, misperception,
     imagination, unconscious state, and memory, while
     $p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack$ defines the ontology aspect distribution
     spanning symbolic aspects from Karma to Brahman. These
     distributions serve as multimodal inputs to the symbolic reasoning
     framework and integrate with the scoring and recursion mechanisms.
     This extension enables unified symbolic reasoning across
     heterogeneous modalities while maintaining consistent symbolic
     entropy measures $(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$ and governance gating across
     multimodal contexts.

168. Where**:** $x_{m}$: encoded feature vector for modality $m$;
     $\phi_{m}$: modality-specific encoder; ${\widetilde{x}}_{m}$:
     normalized modality embedding; $q_{m}$: reliability score of
     modality $m$; $\alpha_{m}$: normalized fusion weight; $h$: fused
     multimodal state vector; $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$: Vritti
     probability for state $v$; $p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack$: ontology aspect
     weight for aspect $a$; $W_{v},W_{a},b_{v},b_{a}$: projection
     parameters; $\kappa$: scaling parameter for reliability weighting.

169. **Personalization**

170. In one embodiment, the system maintains historical user interaction
     data to dynamically adjust Vritti probabilities
     $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ and aspect weights $p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack$.
     Historical distributions are aggregated through an exponential
     moving average:

$${\bar{p}}_{v}^{(t)} = (1 - \lambda)\,{\bar{p}}_{v}^{(t - 1)} + \lambda\, p_{v}^{(t)}$$

$${\bar{p}}_{w}^{(t)} = (1 - \lambda)\,{\bar{p}}_{w}^{(t - 1)} + \lambda\, p_{w}^{(t)}$$

where $\lambda \in (0,1\rbrack$ is the adaptation rate. The personalized
distributions ${\bar{p}}_{v}^{(t)}$ and ${\bar{p}}_{w}^{(t)}$ are then
used in place of $p_{v}$ and $p_{w}$ within the relevance score defined
in claim \[2\].

171. Entropy thresholds are also adapted according to the historical
     variance of interactions. A dynamic readiness threshold $\tau_{t}$
     is defined as:

$$\tau_{t} = \tau_{0} \cdot \left( 1 + \gamma \cdot \sigma_{H}^{(t)} \right)$$

172. where $\tau_{0}$ is a base entropy threshold, $\sigma_{H}^{(t)}$ is
     the running variance of entropy measures $(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$, and
     $\gamma$ is a scaling factor. When $\tau_{t}$ increases due to high
     historical variability, recursion depth is reduced or stabilized by
     switching to experiential anchors; when $\tau_{t}$ decreases,
     deeper recursion is permitted.

173. This embodiment allows the system to adapt symbolically to
     individual user patterns, providing stable yet personalized
     recursion flows and mitigating symbolic distortion over time.

174. Where $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$: Vritti probability for state $v$.;
     $p_{w}\lbrack a\rbrack$: ontology aspect weight for aspect $a$;
     ${\bar{p}}_{v}^{(t)},{\bar{p}}_{w}^{(t)}$: personalized historical
     averages ; $\lambda$: adaptation rate for updating; distributions;
     $\tau_{0}$: base entropy threshold; $\tau_{t}$: personalized
     entropy threshold at time $t$; $\sigma_{H}^{(t)}$: running variance
     of entropy values $H_{D},H_{G},H_{K}$; $\gamma$: scaling parameter
     for entropy adjustment.

175. **Distributed recursion**

176. In one embodiment, recursion states $x^{(k)}$ defined in
     claim \[2\] are partitioned across multiple processors or nodes,
     each computing a local update:

$$x_{j}^{(k + 1)} = f\left( x_{j}^{(k)},\, p_{v}^{(j)},\, p_{w}^{(j)},\, H^{(j)} \right),$$

177. where $j$ indexes the processor or node. Local Vritti distributions
     $p_{v}^{(j)}$ and aspect weights $p_{w}^{(j)}$ are periodically
     synchronized by averaging:

$$p_{v}^{(\text{sync})}(v) = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{j = 1}^{M}p_{v}^{(j)}(v),\quad\quad p_{w}^{(\text{sync})}(a) = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{j = 1}^{M}p_{w}^{(j)}(a),$$

178. for $M$ participating nodes. Entropy measures are similarly
     aggregated:

$$H^{(\text{sync})} = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{j = 1}^{M}H^{(j)}.$$

179. The synchronized values
     $(p_{v}^{(\text{sync})},p_{w}^{(\text{sync})},H^{(\text{sync})})$
     are then redistributed to each node, ensuring consistent recursion
     depth and stability across the network.

180. Where $x_{j}^{(k)}$: recursion state on node $j$ at iteration $k$;
     $f( \cdot )$: recursion update function; $p_{v}^{(j)}$: local
     Vritti probability distribution on node $j$; $p_{w}^{(j)}$: local
     aspect weight distribution on node $j$; $H^{(j)}$: local entropy
     measures $(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$ on node $j$; $M$: number of
     participating nodes; $p_{v}^{(\text{sync})},p_{w}^{(\text{sync})}$:
     synchronized global distributions; $H^{(\text{sync})}$:
     synchronized global entropy values.

181. A user-facing interface may be implemented as follows. In one
     embodiment, the system generates visual or graphical displays of
     internal symbolic state variables to improve transparency and
     interpretability. The interface renders entropy measures
     $(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$ over time, recursion depth $k$ during
     iterative processing, and governance gate outcomes $g_{i}$ for
     candidate responses.

182. Where: $(H_{D},H_{G},H_{K})$: dimensional, guna, and kosha entropy
     values; $k$: recursion depth index; $g_{i}$: governance gate
     outcome for candidate $i$ (binary admit/reject).

183. Example metrics displayed may include: -- Entropy trajectories,
     showing stability or fluctuation across symbolic layers. --
     Recursion depth counters, displaying the current iteration $k$ and
     maximum depth allowed. -- Governance gate results
     $g_{i} \in \{ 0,1\}$, indicating whether a candidate was admitted
     or filtered at each stage. This visualization layer allows
     end-users or developers to observe symbolic reasoning dynamics,
     diagnose instability, and validate alignment with expected
     behaviors.

184. Illustrative Embodiments

185. In one embodiment, the system is applied to a physical reasoning
     scenario that traditional probabilistic language models fail to
     resolve. For example, consider the query: *"If an object is placed
     on a table and the table is pushed, what happens to the object?"*
     Conventional text-trained LLM models lack grounded knowledge of
     dependency relations and may not infer that the object moves with
     the table unless this relationship is explicitly stated in training
     data. By contrast, the disclosed system resolves the scenario
     symbolically by mapping the object--surface relation into ontology
     aspects (Form and Identity), representing the push action at the
     Execution layer, and applying force dynamics in which inertia
     represents resistance to change, propulsion represents motion
     imparted by the push, and coherence represents maintenance of
     relative alignment between dependent forms. These dynamics
     collectively determine whether the object remains on the surface or
     slips. The Physical Kosha further validates manifestation, with
     entropy feedback indicating the lowest-distortion inference
     path---namely, that the object is displaced along with the table.
     This embodiment illustrates how recursive ontology flows, force
     dynamics, and layered validation enable common sense causal
     inference independent of textual co-occurrence.

186. In another embodiment, the system is applied to a societal query
     requiring symbolic reasoning. For example, consider the input:
     *"The world feels chaotic and polarized right now. How can we, as a
     species, find our way back to a more balanced and harmonious
     state?"* A conventional probabilistic LLM model typically generates
     linear, pragmatic suggestions such as empathy, education, or
     discourse. By contrast, the disclosed system performs recursive
     symbolic analysis. The system first identifies the emotionally
     charged energy words "chaotic," "polarized," "balanced," and
     "harmonious," and decomposes them into syllabic units with
     associated vrittis. It then detects a folded truth, namely that
     polarization reflects not only external social conflict but also an
     underlying dissonance between the Thinking layer (desire and fear)
     and the Purposing layer (direction and meaning). The system further
     computes inner and outer flows, wherein the inner flow corresponds
     to reconciliation between Thinking and Purposing, and the outer
     flow corresponds to alignment between Body and Society. Through
     force-dynamic diagnosis, the system identifies overactive
     propulsion (restless division) as the dominant distortion and
     suppressed coherence (stability and clarity) as the missing
     quality. Finally, the Delivery Harmonization Algorithm modulates
     the output tone based on entropy and readiness, producing
     alternative responses such as: (i) a Sweet Resonance tone, which
     restores calm by amplifying coherence; (ii) an Inverse Jolt tone,
     which interrupts restlessness by enforcing inertia; or (iii) a
     Symbolic Metaphor tone, which reframes rigidity into constructive
     motion (for example, describing humanity as an instrument that must
     be tuned). This embodiment illustrates how the system generates
     symbolic, resonance-aligned responses that diagnose distortions,
     map inner and outer flows, and adjust delivery through
     force-dynamic balancing --- capabilities not achievable with
     linear, text-only systems.

187. The foregoing embodiments are provided for illustrative purposes
     only and are not intended to be limiting. Variations in
     application, domain, and expression of the disclosed system will be
     apparent to those skilled in the art. The principles described
     herein --- including recursive ontology flows, force-dynamic
     modulation, layered validation, and entropy feedback --- may be
     applied to physical reasoning, symbolic reasoning, or other domains
     requiring causal inference beyond probabilistic text correlation.
     Accordingly, the scope of the invention is defined by the appended
     claims, rather than the specific examples described.

188. Detailed Description of The Drawings

189. FIG. 1B illustrates a network diagram for symbolic reasoning with
     resonance in the LLMs implemented over a blockchain network
     consistent with the present disclosure.

190. Referring to FIG. 1B, the example network 100' includes the
     Symbolic Reasoning (SR) Node 102 connected to a cloud server
     node(s) 105 over a network. The SR node 102 is configured to host a
     Candidate Generation Module 107. The SR node 102 may receive input
     text 202 data from user entities 111. The example network 100
     supports a symbolic language model (Symbol-U) that integrates
     syllabic decomposition, ontology mapping, and entropy feedback to
     improve stability, explainability, and grounding in large language
     models (LLMs). The input text 202 is decomposed into syllables,
     each associated with Vritti (mental state) distributions such as
     valid cognition, imagination, misperception, unconscious state, and
     memory. Units are then mapped into a ten-layer ontology, and
     dimensional and Guna entropies are computed to assess stability and
     symbolic alignment. A stitching encoder scores candidate responses
     with penalties, while runtime toggles regulate thresholds and tonal
     harmonization through a Delivery Harmonization Algorithm (DHA).
     Recursive ontology flows provide self-correction, while
     experiential anchors and a Deferred Insight Engine resurface
     unresolved content under readiness conditions. Governance gates
     filter outputs, and recursion states and entropy values are
     recorded in an append-only log for transparency and compliance. The
     candidate responses may be stored locally in the data storage 103
     or remotely on the cloud storage 106. Exchanges of users'
     confidential and private information may be implemented over a
     permissioned blockchain network for security and anonymity as
     discussed in more details below.

191. In one embodiment, the SR node 102 may store (or retrieve if
     needed) candidate responses on a permissioned blockchain 110 ledger
     109 based on a consensus from the node(s) 102 and user nodes. As
     discussed above, Tamper-Resistant Logging may be also implemented
     using blockchain ledger 109. In some embodiments, the
     explainability log is further secured against tampering using
     cryptographic chaining, digital signatures, blockchain-based
     distributed ledger 109, thereby ensuring immutability and
     preventing retroactive alteration of logged variables. Blockchain
     110 implementations may store each log entry as a block linked to
     the previous one ensuring decentralized immutability and
     auditability. These mechanisms prevent retroactive alteration of
     recorded variables and provide verifiable proof of integrity
     compliance and provenance.

192. In this implementation, the SR node 102, the cloud server 105, the
     user entity nodes 111 may serve as blockchain 110 peer nodes. In
     one embodiment, local data from the database 103 and remote data
     from the database 106 may be duplicated on the blockchain ledger
     109 for higher security of storage.

