263. With reference to FIG. 4B, at block 434 the processor 204 may, if
     things get unstable, switch from symbolic recursion to experiential
     anchor reasoning. Switch back when stable. The processor 204 may
     dynamically switch between symbolic recursion and experiential
     anchor recursion when instability is detected, wherein hybrid
     switching ensures continuity of reasoning by detouring into
     experiential anchors during high-entropy states and resuming
     symbolic recursion upon stabilization; wherein the switching
     function is defined as:

$$\ \ Mode(t) = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
\text{Symbolic}, & \text{if }H_{D} \leq \tau_{D}\mspace{6mu}\mspace{6mu}\text{and}\mspace{6mu}\mspace{6mu} H_{G} \leq \tau_{G}, \\
\text{Anchor}, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ \ $$

264. where $H_{D}$ is dimensional entropy, $H_{G}$ is Guna entropy,
     $\tau_{D}$ and $\tau_{G}$ are respective thresholds, and $Mode(t)$
     indicates the recursion mode at time $t$.

265. At block 436 the processor 204 may blend outer practical answers
     with inner deep recursion insights. Adjust the blend with a tunable
     parameter.

266. The processor 204 may generate dual reasoning flows, wherein the
     outer flow provides execution-aligned responses for practical tasks
     and the inner flow traverses all reasoning layers recursively to
     surface latent insights; wherein the flows are computed in parallel
     such that

$$\text{Output} = \alpha \cdot \text{OuterFlow} + (1 - \alpha) \cdot \text{InnerFlow}$$

267. where $OuterFlow$ represents responses aligned with immediate
     execution, $InnerFlow$ represents recursive traversal through
     ontology layers, and $\alpha \in \lbrack 0,1\rbrack$ is a tunable
     blending parameter controlling emphasis between execution utility
     and recursive depth.

268. At block 438, the processor 204 may stop recursion when the system
     stabilizes (little change across steps) or when a maximum number of
     iterations is reached.

269. The processor 204 may terminate recursion when stability is
     achieved, wherein convergence is detected by measuring changes in
     recursion states across iterations; wherein the convergence
     criterion is defined as

$$\Delta(x^{(k)}) = \left\| x^{(k)} - x^{(k - 1)} \right\|_{2} < \epsilon$$

270. where $x^{(k)}$ is the recursion state at iteration $k$,
     $x^{(k - 1)}$ is the recursion state at the previous iteration,
     $\Delta(x^{(k)})$ is the change in state, and $\epsilon$ is a
     predefined stability. threshold.

271. At block 440, the processor 204 may map the inputs including audio,
     visual, or tags into Vritti s and ontology aspects the same way as
     texts.

272. The processor 204 may use the input tokens including multimodal
     features including acoustic signals, visual features, symbolic
     tags, or semantic embeddings, and wherein such features are mapped
     into Vritti distributions and ontology aspects for unified symbolic
     reasoning across text, audio, and image modalities.

273. At block 442, the processor 204 may adjust weights and thresholds
     based on past user interactions. This keeps the system stable and
     tailored to the user.

274. The processor 204 may dynamically adjust Vritti probabilities and
     aspect weights based on historical user interaction data, wherein
     personalization is achieved by modifying recursion flows and
     adapting entropy thresholds to maintain stability and reduce
     symbolic distortion over time.

275. At block 444, the processor 204 may distribute recursion across
     multiple processors or nodes. Synchronize entropy and Vritti values
     to keep everything consistent across nodes. The recursion
     computations may be distributed across multiple processors or
     networked nodes, and wherein entropy values and Vrittiprobabilities
     are synchronized to maintain symbolic consistency.

276. At block 446, the processor 204 may provide a user-facing dashboard
     that shows entropy, recursion depth, and gate results. This makes
     reasoning transparent for developers, auditors, and users.

277. The candidate responses may be stored in a centralized local
     database (such as one used for storing local data 103 depicted in
     FIGs. 1A-B).

278. In another embodiment, the SR node 102 may use a decentralized
     storage such as a blockchain 110 (see FIG. 1B), which is a
     distributed storage system comprising multiple nodes that
     communicate with each other. The decentralized storage includes an
     append-only immutable data structure resembling a distributed
     ledger 109, capable of maintaining records between mutually
     untrusted parties. The untrusted parties are referred to herein as
     peers or peer nodes. Each peer maintains a copy of the records, and
     no single peer can modify the records without consensus being
     reached among the distributed peers.\
     For example, the peers 102 and 105 (FIG. 1B) may execute a
     consensus protocol to validate blockchain 110 storage transactions,
     group the storage transactions into blocks, and build a hash chain
     over the blocks. This process forms the ledger 109 by ordering the
     storage transactions for consistency.\
     In various embodiments, a permissioned and/or a permissionless
     blockchain may be used. In a public or permissionless blockchain,
     anyone may participate without a specific identity. Public
     blockchains may involve assets and use consensus protocols such as
     Proof of Work (PoW). By contrast, a permissioned blockchain
     provides secure interactions among a group of entities that share a
     common goal (such as storing candidate responses) but do not fully
     trust one another.

279. This application utilizes a permissioned (private) blockchain that
     operates arbitrary programmable logic tailored to a decentralized
     storage scheme, referred to as "smart contracts" or "chaincodes."
     In some embodiments, specialized chaincodes may exist for
     management functions and parameters, herein referred to as system
     chaincodes.\
     The application may further utilize smart contracts as trusted
     distributed applications that leverage the tamper-proof properties
     of the blockchain database and an underlying agreement between
     nodes, which is referred to as an endorsement or endorsement
     policy. Blockchain transactions associated with this application
     may be "endorsed" before being committed to the blockchain, while
     transactions that are not endorsed are disregarded.\
     An endorsement policy allows chaincodes to specify endorsers for a
     transaction in the form of a set of peer nodes that are necessary
     for endorsement. When a client sends the transaction to the peers
     specified in the endorsement policy, the transaction is executed to
     validate the transaction. After validation, the transactions enter
     an ordering phase in which a consensus protocol is applied to
     produce an ordered sequence of endorsed transactions grouped into
     blocks.

