299. A system consistent with an embodiment of the disclosure, the
     computing device 500 may include the clock module 510, known to a
     person having ordinary skill in the art as a clock generator, which
     produces clock signals. A clock signal oscillates between high and
     low states and is used to coordinate actions of digital circuits.
     Most integrated circuits (ICs) of sufficient complexity use a clock
     signal to synchronize different parts of the circuit. The
     preeminent example is the CPU 520, the central component of modern
     computers, which relies on a clock. In particular embodiments, the
     clock module 510 may further synchronize recursion cycles across
     ontology layers, trigger entropy evaluations, and align deferred
     insight resurfacing with readiness thresholds. In high-performance
     configurations, the clock module may coordinate CPU--GPU/NPU
     interaction via PCIe, NVLink, or CXL, ensuring stable symbolic
     reasoning flows.

300. Many computing devices 500 use a "clock multiplier" which
     multiplies a lower frequency external clock to the appropriate
     clock rate of the CPU 520. This allows the CPU 520 to operate at a
     much higher frequency than the rest of the computer, which affords
     performance gains in situations where the CPU 520 does not need to
     wait on an external factor (like memory 540 or input/output 560).
     Some embodiments of the clock 510 may include dynamic frequency
     change, where the time between clock edges vary widely from one
     edge to the next and back again. In certain embodiments, dynamic
     clocking may also be used to modulate symbolic recursion depth, to
     accelerate entropy feedback loops, or to slow cycles for deferred
     insight resurfacing until readiness conditions are satisfied. Thus,
     adaptive frequency scaling can serve both hardware efficiency and
     symbolic reasoning stability in the disclosed framework.

