# CLAIMS

\[1\] A method for symbolic reasoning in a language model, comprising:

\(a\) a processor of a symbolic reasoning (SR) node configured to host a
candidate generation module and communicatively coupled to at least one
user entity node over a network; and

\(b\) a non-transitory memory storing machine-readable instructions
which, when executed by the processor, cause the processor to: (i)
decompose an input text received from the at least one user entity node
into syllabic or phonetic units; (ii) associate each unit with one or
more mental state distributions comprising Vrittis; (iii) map the units
into an ontology of reasoning layers; (iv) compute entropy values over
symbolic aspects, Guna states, and Koshas to assess stability; and (v)
generate one or more candidate responses using the candidate generation
module.

\[2\] The method of claim \[1\], further comprising computing, for each
candidate response, a relevance score that balances symbolic alignment
and contextual fit, wherein the score ensures candidates are prioritized
based on aspect probability, resonance with Vritti states, domain
suitability, template coherence, and factual confidence; wherein the
relevance score is computed with penalties for redundancy and domain
jumps as:

$${rel}_{i} = (p_{w}\lbrack a_{i}\rbrack)^{\theta_{1}} \cdot \left( \sum_{v \in V_{\text{Vritti }}}^{}p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack \cdot R\lbrack v,a_{i}\rbrack \right)^{\theta_{2}} \cdot \phi_{d}(d_{i} \mid a_{i})^{\theta_{3}} \cdot \phi_{t}(t_{i} \mid p_{v})^{\theta_{4}} \cdot c^{\theta_{5}},$$

where $p_{w}\lbrack a_{i}\rbrack$ is the probability of aspect $a_{i}$
for candidate $i$, $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ is the probability of Vritti
state $v$, $R\lbrack v,a_{i}\rbrack$ is a resonance coupling
coefficient, $\phi_{d}( \cdot )$ is a domain adjustment function,
$\phi_{t}( \cdot )$ is a template adjustment function, $c$ is a
fact-confidence coefficient, and $\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{5}$ are
tunable exponents.

\[3\] The method of claim \[2\], further comprising selecting an ordered
subset of candidate responses using an objective function that maximizes
relevance and applies penalties for redundancy and domain shifts, the
objective function being expressible as:

$$S^{*} = arg\max_{S,\, order}\left( \sum_{i \in S}^{}{rel}_{i} - \lambda_{1}\, red(S) - \lambda_{2}\, dj(S) \right),$$

subject to $|S| \leq K$, $len \leq L$, and ${rel}_{i} \geq \tau_{rel}$,
where $\lambda_{1}$ is a redundancy penalty, $\lambda_{2}$ is a
domain-jump penalty, and $\tau_{rel}$ and $L$ are predefined parameters.

\[4\] The method of claim \[3\], further comprising applying penalties
to discourage redundancy and abrupt domain transitions, wherein the
penalties ensure candidate subsets remain diverse and contextually
smooth; wherein redundancy is computed as:

$$red(S) = \sum_{i < j \in S}^{}\left( \alpha_{sem} \cdot cos(e_{i},e_{j}) + \alpha_{asp} \cdot 1(a_{i} = a_{j}) + \alpha_{tmp} \cdot 1(t_{i} = t_{j}) \right),$$

and domain-jump penalties are computed as

$$dj(S) = \sum_{i \rightarrow j \in order(S)}^{}D(d_{i},d_{j}),$$

where $cos(e_{i},e_{j})$ measures semantic similarity between candidate
responses, $1( \cdot )$ is an indicator function, and $D(d_{i},d_{j})$
is a baseline or reference domain distance; it being understood that
other equivalent penalty formulations may also be employed, including
distance-based, probabilistic, or entropy-weighted measures.

\[5\] The method of claim \[3\], further comprising activating runtime
hotfix toggles that adaptively adjust thresholds and quality metrics to
improve efficiency and stability under varying entropy conditions;
wherein an adjusted quality score $Q^{'}$ is computed as:

$$Q^{'} = Q_{\text{base}} + bump,$$

and a hedging threshold $\tau_{\text{hedge}}$ is computed as:

$$\tau_{\text{hedge}} = clip(\alpha + \beta H_{D},\,\tau_{\min},\,\tau_{\max}),$$

where $Q_{\text{base}}$ is a baseline quality measure, $bump$ is an
adjustment term, $H_{D}$ is a dimensional entropy value, and
$\alpha,\beta,\tau_{\min},\tau_{\max}$ are tunable parameters.

wherein expanded candidate generation is triggered when entropy values
exceed thresholds, such that:

$$Trigger = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1, & \text{if }H_{D} > \tau_{D}\mspace{6mu}\mspace{6mu}\text{or}\mspace{6mu}\mspace{6mu} H_{G} > \tau_{G}, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\ $$

where $H_{D}$ and $H_{G}$ are dimensional and Guna entropy values, and
$\tau_{D},\tau_{G}$ are thresholds; and wherein a Delivery Harmonization
Algorithm modulates the tone of outputs, selecting among Sweet
Resonance, Inverse Jolt, or Symbolic Metaphor delivery modes based on
entropy values and readiness scores.

\[6\] The method of claim \[1\], wherein the Vritti distributions
underlying the probabilities $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ referenced in claim
\[2\], are explicitly grounded in classical categories of mental states,
the Vrittis comprising valid cognition, imagination, misperception,
unconscious state, and memory.

\[7\] The method of claim \[1\], further comprising performing recursion
within sub-layers nested inside reasoning layers, wherein sub-layer
recursion provides fractal refinement of symbolic reasoning and improves
stability under complex inputs; wherein a recursion state is updated
according to:

$$x^{(k + 1)} = f\left( x^{(k)},\, L_{\text{sub}},\, H_{D},\, H_{G} \right),$$

where $x^{(k)}$ is the recursion state at iteration $k$,
$L_{\text{sub}}$ is a nested sub-layer within a reasoning layer, and
$H_{D}$ and $H_{G}$ are the dimensional and Guna entropy values,
respectively.

\[8\] The method of claim \[1\], further comprising deferring unresolved
symbolic content into a deferred-insight store, wherein each deferred is
tracked with lifecycle flags including $is\_ active$ and
$is\_ ready\_ to\_ surface$, and resurfacing occurs when a readiness
score $R$ satisfies

$$R = \alpha H_{D} + \beta H_{G} + \gamma H_{K} + \delta T \geq \tau_{\text{ready}}$$

where $H_{D},H_{G},H_{K}$ are the dimensional, Guna, and Kosha entropy
values, $T$ is elapsed time since deferral, $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta$
are weighting parameters, and $\tau_{\text{ready}}$ is a readiness
threshold; and wherein, in some embodiments, readiness further
incorporates mirrored entropy or domain entropy gating to ensure
stability across symbolic layers; and wherein resurfacing is further
governed by a triadic balancing framework comprising: (i) a unifying
force that applies progressive pressure to surface deferred insights,
(ii) an observing restraint that defers resurfacing until readiness
thresholds are satisfied, and (iii) a mirror logic that reflects
insights in symbolic, partial, or preview form prior to full delivery,
wherein the framework ensures deferred truths are neither prematurely
suppressed nor destructively surfaced.

\[9\] The method of claim \[1\], further comprising producing dual
reasoning flows, including an outer execution-aligned flow and an inner
recursive flow; computing dimensional entropy $H_{D}$ and Guna entropy
$H_{G}$ for the input; performing recursion across ontology layers and
sub-layers using the computed entropy values; dynamically selecting an
experiential anchor when $H_{D} > \tau_{D}$ or $H_{G} > \tau_{G}$, where
$\tau_{D}$ and $\tau_{G}$ are entropy thresholds; applying governance
gates that evaluate harm, time, resonance, and provenance prior to final
output; and applying a Delivery Harmonization Algorithm that selects a
tonal delivery mode from the group consisting of at least Sweet
Resonance, Inverse Jolt, and Symbolic Metaphor, based on entropy values,
readiness scores, and governance outcomes.

\[10\] The method of claim \[9\], further comprising adjusting recursion
confidence dynamically by applying monotonic, saturating modulation
functions to scale confidence weights for dimensional and Guna entropy
values; wherein adjusted recursion weights $\alpha^{'}$, $\beta^{'}$,
and $\gamma^{'}$ are computed as:

$$\alpha^{'} = \frac{\alpha}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{D} - \tau_{D})}}\quad\quad\beta^{'} = \frac{\beta}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{G} - \tau_{G})}}\quad\quad\gamma^{'} = \frac{\gamma}{1 + e^{- \kappa(H_{K} - \tau_{K})}}$$

where $H_{D}$, $H_{G}$, and $H_{K}$ are the dimensional, Guna, and Kosha
entropy values, $\tau_{D}$, $\tau_{G}$, and $\tau_{K}$ are entropy
thresholds, $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ are base scaling parameters,
and $\kappa$ is a sharpness parameter controlling sigmoid steepness.

\[11\] The method of claim \[9\], further comprising computing a
context--Vritti coupling coefficient $s_{c}$, wherein the coefficient
maintains symbolic meaning aligned with the active semantic context of
the input; wherein the coefficient is computed as:

$$s_{c} = \sum_{v \in V_{\text{Vritti}}}^{}p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack \cdot \kappa(v,C),$$

where $p_{v}\lbrack v\rbrack$ is the probability of Vritti state $v$,
$C$ is a symbolic or semantic context of the input, and $\kappa(v,C)$ is
a similarity function between Vritti state $v$ and context $C$; it being
understood that other equivalent formulations of the coefficient may
also be employed, including similarity measures, kernel functions, or
probabilistic couplings that ensure alignment between Vritti
distributions and contextual features.

\[12\] The method of claim \[9\], further comprising computing a
resonance modulation coefficient $\lambda_{\text{res}}$, wherein the
coefficient dynamically scales recursion confidence based on the balance
of Guna states, thereby stabilizing symbolic reasoning under variable
conditions; wherein the coefficient is computed as:

$$\lambda_{\text{res}} = \frac{\sum_{g \in G}^{}p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack \cdot \rho(g)}{\sum_{g \in G}^{}p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack},$$

where $p_{g}\lbrack g\rbrack$ is the probability weight for Guna state
$g$, $\rho(g)$ is a resonance weight associated with Guna state $g$, and
$G$ is the set of Guna states.

\[13\] The method of claim \[9\], further comprising applying governance
gates to candidate responses, including a harm gate, time gate,
resonance gate, domain entropy gate, cross-domain entropy gate, and
Kosha readiness gate; wherein each gate suppresses or validates
responses according to a threshold, as follows:

$$\begin{aligned}
G_{\text{harm}}(c) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
0 & \text{if }risk(c) > \tau_{\text{harm}}, \\
1 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{time}}(c) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
0 & \text{if }t_{\text{age}}(c) > \tau_{\text{time}}, \\
1 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{res}}(c) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }\lambda_{\text{res}}(c) \geq \tau_{\text{res}}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{dom}}(a) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }H_{D}(a) \leq \tau_{\text{dom}}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{cross}}(a,b) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }H_{\times}(a,b) \leq \tau_{\text{cross}}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{kosha}}(k) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }R^{K}(k) \geq \tau_{\text{kosha}}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ 
\end{aligned}$$

where $risk(c)$ is a harm-risk score, $t_{\text{age}}(c)$ is elapsed
time, $\lambda_{\text{res}}(c)$ is a resonance score, $H_{D}(a)$ is the
entropy within symbolic domain $a$, $H_{\times}(a,b)$ is the
cross-entropy between domains $a$ and $b$, and $R^{K}(k)$ is the
readiness score of Kosha $k$; and
$\tau_{\text{harm}},\tau_{\text{time}},\tau_{\text{res}},\tau_{\text{dom}},\tau_{\text{cross}},\tau_{\text{kosha}}$
are respective thresholds; it being understood that other equivalent
gating functions may also be employed to enforce substantially the same
suppress-or-validate behavior.

\[14\] The method of claim \[13\], further comprising applying extended
governance gates including a provenance gate and a compliance gate;
wherein the provenance gate validates that a candidate response
originates from an approved source, and the compliance gate enforces
policy or regulatory constraints on candidate responses; wherein the
gates are computed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
G_{\text{prov}}(c) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }source(c) \in S_{\text{approved}}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{matrix} \right.\  \\
G_{\text{comp}}(c) & = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
1 & \text{if }policy(c)\text{ satisfies compliance constraints}, \\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{matrix} \right.\ 
\end{aligned}$$

where $source(c)$ is a provenance identifier for candidate $c$,
$S_{\text{approved}}$ is a set of approved sources, and $policy(c)$
represents metadata or rules applicable to candidate $c$; it being
understood that other equivalent formulations of provenance validation
and compliance enforcement may also be employed to achieve substantially
the same effect.

\[15\] The method of claim \[1\], further comprising recording an
explainability log at each recursion step $t$, wherein the log provides
transparency and compliance by capturing internal state variables and
verifying their integrity over time; wherein the log includes
dimensional entropy $H_{D}$, Guna entropy $H_{G}$, penalty weights
$\lambda_{1}$ and $\lambda_{2}$, complexity metric $Q$, template
parameter $\beta$, hedging threshold $\tau_{\text{hedge}}$, and anomaly
score $a(t)$; wherein the anomaly score is computed as:

$$a(t) = \left\| x(t) - \widehat{x}(t) \right\|_{2},$$

where $x(t)$ is the current state vector of logged variables and
$\widehat{x}(t)$ is an exponential moving average of past state vectors;
and further comprising appending a provenance tuple\
$\langle model_{ver},ver_{set},data_{stamp}\rangle$ and a hash
$H_{t}^{*} = h(H_{D},H_{G},R_{t})$, where $R_{t}$ is the serialized
record at step $t$, such that the log establishes an auditable chain of
records; it being understood that other equivalent anomaly scoring
functions may also be employed, including probabilistic likelihood
ratios, kernel-based distance measures, or entropy-derived thresholds.

\[16\] The method of claim \[15\], wherein the explainability log is
