\[17\] The method of claim \[3\], further comprising dynamically
updating aspect weights when instability is detected, wherein the update
rule improves stability by reinforcing supportive evidence while
reducing redundancy and domain-jump penalties; wherein the update is
computed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
P_{w}^{(t + 1)}\lbrack a\rbrack = normalize( & P_{w}^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack^{\gamma} \cdot e^{\eta U^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack} \cdot e^{- \mu_{1}{Redundancy}^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack} \cdot e^{- \mu_{2}{DomainJump}^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack}).
\end{aligned}$$

where $P_{w}^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack$ is the aspect weight at step $t$,
$\gamma$ is a scaling exponent, $\eta$ is a learning rate, $\mu_{1}$ and
$\mu_{2}$ are penalty weights, and $U^{(t)}\lbrack a\rbrack$ is a
utility term comprising evidence scores, Vritti--aspect coupling, and
resonance from other aspects; it being understood that other equivalent
update functions may also be employed to achieve substantially the same
stabilizing effect.

\[18\] The method of claim \[9\], further comprising modulating
recursion transitions using experiential anchors, wherein the anchors
provide grounding and stability by referencing human experiential
categories during high-entropy states; wherein the experiential anchors
